  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Fundamental Angular Frequency",
    "lesson_core_move": "Identify the fundamental angular frequency $\\omega$_0 as the angular rate of one fundamental cycle for a periodic continuous-time signal and use it as the base spacing for harmonic frequencies.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Angular Frequency",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fundamental Angular Frequency",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a periodic continuous-time signal, start by recognizing the fundamental cycle. The angular frequency attached to that one repeating cycle is $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the simplest base case: one full cycle takes $T_0$ seconds, and over that cycle the phase advances by one full turn, or $2\\pi$ radians. That is why $\\omega_0$ is measured in rad/s: it is the angular rate of the repeating pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This formula gives the phase-advance rate for one cycle. If the signal repeats faster, $\\omega_0$ is larger; if it repeats more slowly, $\\omega_0$ is smaller.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_k = k\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once $\\omega_0$ is set, the harmonic angular frequencies fall on a uniform ladder at integer multiples of the same spacing. So $\\omega_0$ is not just one frequency value; it is the base step size for the whole harmonic set.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep this lesson focused on interpreting $\\omega_0$ as the angular rate of one fundamental cycle and as the spacing between harmonics. Later lessons will connect $\\omega_0$ and $T_0$ in conversion problems, but here the job is to recognize the base rate cleanly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Angular Frequency",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $\\omega$_0 as Angular Rate",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal completes one fundamental cycle every $4$ s. Interpret $\\omega_0$ as the angular rate of that cycle and identify its value.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phrase 'one fundamental cycle every $4$ s.' That tells you to read the cycle time as $T_0 = 4$ s and to interpret $\\omega_0$ as the phase-advance rate of that single repeat.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "One fundamental cycle always means one full $2\\pi$ radians of phase advance. So the angular rate is the phase advance per cycle divided by the cycle time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $T_0 = 4$ s and simplify.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal's fundamental angular frequency is $\\pi/2$ rad/s. In words, the signal advances by $\\pi/2$ radians each second across one repeating cycle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the interpretation to carry forward: $\\omega_0$ is the angular rate attached to one fundamental cycle, not a harmonic multiple or a separate period value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Angular Frequency",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying $\\omega$_0 from One Fundamental Cycle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal repeats once every $\\frac{2}{5}$ s. Find its fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$ exactly.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phrase 'repeats once every $\\frac{2}{5}$ s.' That tells you the fundamental period is $T_0 = \\frac{2}{5}$ s, so the job is to turn one cycle into an angular rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "One fundamental cycle always corresponds to a full $2\\pi$ radians of phase advance. So the base angular frequency is the phase advance per cycle divided by the cycle time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $T_0 = \\frac{2}{5}$ s and simplify exactly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\frac{2}{5}} = 5\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fundamental angular frequency is $5\\pi$ rad/s. The unit check matches the interpretation: radians per second is the angular rate of one repeating cycle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Angular Frequency",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Distinguishing $\\omega$_0 from Harmonic Angular Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has angular frequencies $4$ rad/s, $8$ rad/s, and $12$ rad/s. Which value is $\\omega_0$, and which values are harmonics?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the list of angular frequencies $4$ rad/s, $8$ rad/s, and $12$ rad/s. The job is not to compute a period; it is to identify the base spacing that the other values sit on.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the smallest positive value. Here that is $4$ rad/s, so take $\\omega_0 = 4$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "8 = 2\\omega_0, \\qquad 12 = 3\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because each larger frequency is an integer multiple of $\\omega_0$, both are harmonics. The multiple on $8$ rad/s is $2$, so it is the second harmonic; the multiple on $12$ rad/s is $3$, so it is the third harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the classification is $\\omega_0 = 4$ rad/s, while $8$ rad/s and $12$ rad/s are harmonic frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The key rule to carry forward is: choose the smallest positive spacing as $\\omega_0$, then label only the integer multiples as harmonics. Do not pick the largest value just because it appears later in the list.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Angular Frequency",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Using $\\omega$_0 as Harmonic Frequency Spacing",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If a periodic signal has $\\omega_0 = 3$ rad/s, what are the first four positive harmonic frequencies?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the given base spacing $\\omega_0 = 3$ rad/s. The job is to build the harmonic ladder by multiplying that spacing by consecutive positive integers.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the harmonic rule $\\omega_k = k\\omega_0$ for $k = 1, 2, 3, 4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_k = k\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $\\omega_0 = 3$ rad/s and evaluate the first four terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_1 = 1(3) = 3, \\qquad \\omega_2 = 2(3) = 6, \\qquad \\omega_3 = 3(3) = 9, \\qquad \\omega_4 = 4(3) = 12",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the first four positive harmonic frequencies are $3$ rad/s, $6$ rad/s, $9$ rad/s, and $12$ rad/s. Each one is spaced by $3$ rad/s, which is exactly $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The carry-forward rule is to start at $\\omega_0$ and keep multiplying by consecutive positive integers. Do not divide by the harmonic number or skip the base spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
